<DocsLayout
  slug="builds"
  title="Triggering & managing builds"
  description="Push to build, or trigger by hand — either way, every run is logged."
>
  <h2 id="automatic-builds">Automatic builds on push</h2>
  <p>
    Every push to a project's configured branch starts a build for each platform you've enabled. A
    new push to the same branch cancels the in-flight run for it, so you never end up waiting
    behind a commit you've already superseded.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manual-builds">Manual builds</h2>
  <p>
    Trigger a build from the project dashboard at any time, without needing a new commit — useful
    for re-running a build after fixing a secret, or building an older commit again.
  </p>

  <h2 id="staging-vs-production">Staging vs. production</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Staging</strong> compiles an unsigned Android debug build and an Ad Hoc-signed iOS build,
    installable over the air — see <a href="/docs/ota-installs">installing staging builds</a>. It's
    available on every plan, without limit.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Production</strong> produces a signed release build. It's gated server-side by plan: on
    the Free plan, a production build request is rejected before it ever reaches GitHub, with a clear
    message explaining why. Starter and above can build production. See <a href="/docs/plans"
      >plans &amp; limits</a
    >.
  </p>

  <h2 id="build-history-and-logs">Build history &amp; logs</h2>
  <p>
    Every run keeps its status, duration, commit, branch, and full logs, so you can always tell
    whether last night's release actually shipped — and why, if it didn't.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
